granblue Fantasy Versus is based on a  property that's incredibly popular in  Japan thanks to a successful mobile game  but relatively unknown everywhere else  vs is for all intents and purposes  granblue fantasies debut on the world  stage okay I'm all set  [Music]  developed by Arc System Works known for  its excellent fighting game adaptations  of Dragon Ball Z and persona 4 grand  blue Fantasy Versus has a strong core  thanks to unorthodox gameplay mechanics  that delicately balance depth with  approach ability while introducing  interesting new ideas of its own the  extra flourishes that serve as a nod for  fans or aim to adhere to the RPG routes  with on occasion but the experience as a  whole hold its own thanks to the  strength of its fundamentals as a  ground-based fighting game versus has a  much slower pace of play than most anime  fighters it places a heavier focus on  normals and special moves instead of  partner assists and lengthy touch of  death combos at a higher level  it's about footsies precisely execute  mix ups smart use of the universal  overhead and the occasional frame trap  for newcomers of which there's likely to  be many given the popularity of granblue  fantasy it's a much more stable ground  to find for you fighting game veterans  will naturally have an advantage but for  everyone else the mountain doesn't seem  as steep to climb Auto combos a common  mechanic in access games have been  included which means that a complete  newcomer can pick up a controller and  press an attack bar repeatedly to  execute basic combos these strings of  attacks are effective but limited in  damage and applicability without the  addition of a special move to the string  and is here that granblue Fantasy Versus  starts to throw in unique ideas that  make for interesting moment-to-moment  fighting special moves are executed  using a traditional combination of  directional inputs and attack button  presses but there are also shortcuts  mapped to R 1 or R 1 + ur direction the  added layer to this is like an RPG each  of these special moves goes on a  cool-down after being used with the  length of that cooldown being longer if  you take a shortcut to execute this  system creates an interesting meta game  that lends a degree of unpredictability  to fights especially at higher levels in  most traditional fighting games there  are certain movements that serve as  tells giving keen-eyed players some  indication of what's about to happen but  that's not the case in granblue fantasy  vs. however since the shortcut commands  can instantly execute special moves it  sounds like a nightmare to deal with and  admittedly it can be but also creates a  fascinating dynamic that hasn't been  explored much in the past the cooldown  system layered on top of these shortcuts  presents  strategic decision-making moment where  you weigh up the pros and cons of using  shortcuts versus having an ability  temporarily taken out of play and how  long it's unavailable for there are also  multiple options for guarding if  directional inputs are used to manually  guard just defenses available with  proper timing however if the dedicated  god button is used it becomes possible  to dash out the dedicated God bar and  also makes blocking cross-ups more  consistent but just defense becomes  unavailable again granblue fantasy vs.  asks you to weigh up whether you want to  take the easy approach or put in the  work for more of a payout what I've  enjoyed most is engaging with the  technical side of play when I feel  confident but then switching to the  simpler options when I'm feeling  overwhelmed or want to be safer that  process of trying to hone the skills  needed to do that effectively has been  incredibly gratifying all that is to say  that fundamentally granblue Fantasy  Versus has a robust and intricate set of  fighting game mechanics all right  another victory for me it's a shame then  that the game doesn't leverage this for  its RPG quest mode and instead relies on  shallow button mashing that was in this  mode look and feel more like the mobile  RPG version of granblue fantasy but it's  not turn-based instead you have direct  control over characters and must  dispatch waves of incoming enemies in  something that feels somewhere between a  classic side-scrolling beat'em up and  Tekken's force mode however the enemies  present little to no challenge and have  very little health so they can usually  be killed off with a couple of heavy  attacks as a result there's little  reason to use some of granblue fantasy  versus more interesting ideas why use a  special move when spamming a single  attack bond gets the job done  occasionally boss characters appear but  they don't force change in approach the  all-out attack is the smartest and  easiest thing to do most of the time  other members of the fighting games cost  occasionally appear and in these account  battles take the form of a traditional  typical one-on-one fight however these  characters are mostly there to be  sandbagged so they can be added to your  party  the unsatisfying gameplay makes the  cliche forgettable story about a bunch  of sky pirates freeing their friends  from evil and battling an empire even  more tiresome to sit through  oh goodness sake the RPG quest mode is  also where the grand balloon fantasy  mobile games data elements appear you  unlock unique weapons for completing the  main story quests but you don't actually  get to use any of them instead they all  get thrown into what is effectively a  bucket and their combined power then  raises the attack attributes of your  character enemies have elemental  alignments and using opposing ones will  grant bonus damage but manually crafting  loadout is time-consuming and  unnecessary when an option to automate  and optimize exists  ultimately the gotcha elements end up  being a throwaway novelty instead of a  valuable wrinkle to gameplay granblue  Fantasy Versus has a comprehensive suite  of training missions that cover  everything from basic moves and specials  to advanced combos strategies and  specific matchups any and all time spent  there is more valuable than in the RPG  quest mode especially since that's what  will be tested when you head online  currently the game servers aren't as  populated as they will be post launch  but in the time we spent playing online  the experience was generally smooth  granblue Fantasy Versus presents a set  of systems that are distinct and unique  without putting up unnecessary hurdles  for those that are there just to get a  hit of granblue Arc System Works has  cleverly meshed RPG and fighting game  mechanics and the way that manifests in  the core one-on-one battles is really  exciting in other respects namely the  campaign story mode and its gacha  stylings leaves a lot to be desired in  bringing granblue to the wider world Arc  System Works hasn't put his best foot  forward but vs. is definitely a game  where you can come for the fantasy and  stay for the fight  [Music]
 

